course: religion-ethics
lesson: 55
title: When a small pet dies: fish, hamsters and budgies
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When a pet dies, its body has stopped working for good: it is not sleeping, it will not swim, run or sing again, and it is almost never anyone's fault.
explain: Picture Ollie coming down for breakfast to find his goldfish, Captain, floating on his side at the top of the tank; his dad tells him the truth gently, they say goodbye together, and they bury Captain in the garden.
explain: Sadness for a pet is real grief, because a pet is a friend you saw every day; nobody should say «it's just a bird», and it is normal to feel sad, angry, or to want someone to blame.
explain: Grown-ups sometimes say «gone to a better place» or «gone to sleep», but clear words help most: the pet has died, its body is still here, and it will not come back to life.
explain: Nobody on earth knows for sure what happens to animals after they die: some Christians believe God will make all creation new, others say heaven is for people, many Hindus and Buddhists believe in rebirth, and others say animals simply die; «I don't know, but you were loved» is an honest answer.
fact: When a pet dies, its body has stopped working completely; it is not asleep and will not wake.
fact: Fish often die suddenly, and often nobody can tell why.
fact: Hamsters live about two or three years, so a hamster of two and a half is old; old hamsters often die quietly in their sleep.
fact: Flushing a dead fish can spread illness to wild fish, and many families prefer to bury a pet in the garden.
fact: Grief for a pet is real grief, and nobody should tell you how sad to be.
fact: One extra pinch of food is very unlikely to kill a fish; an honest mistake is not the same as meaning harm.
fact: «Gone to a better place» and «gone to sleep» are soft ways of saying «died»; clear words help children most.
fact: Hibernation is a deep winter rest that tortoises, hedgehogs and some other animals take: the animal still breathes slowly and its heart still beats; a vet can check if you are worried.
fact: When someone is sad, anger and blame can come out; it is better to ask calmly what happened.
fact: Christians differ about whether animals go to heaven; many Hindus and Buddhists believe animals are reborn; some people believe animals simply die.
fact: In a class, not everyone prays; those who pray can, and others can have a quiet moment or think kind thoughts.
fact: The Bible says Christians should «mourn with those who mourn», even for small losses.
exercise: Ollie finds Captain floating on his side. Is Captain sleeping? | No, Captain has died and will not swim again | Yes, fish always sleep floating at the top of the tank | Yes, he will wake when the light goes on | No, he is only resting after a long swim
exercise: Ollie asks if they can flush Captain. Why might his dad prefer a burial? | Flushing can spread illness, and a burial feels kinder | Toilets are only for living fish | Flushing is against church law | A buried fish will grow into a new fish
exercise: Chloe's old hamster Nibbles died curled up in his bedding. Was he likely scared or in pain? | Probably not; old hamsters often die peacefully in their sleep | Yes, hamsters always die in great pain | Yes, because Chloe was not there | No, because hamsters cannot feel anything at all, ever, in their lives
exercise: Leo's brother says, «It's just a bird, get over it.» What is true? | Grief for a pet is real, and nobody should say how sad to be | His brother is right, birds don't matter as much as cats and dogs | Leo should be sad for exactly one day | Being sad for a pet is babyish
exercise: Ava gave her fish extra food on Saturday. Did she kill it? | Very unlikely; often nobody knows why fish die | Yes, one extra pinch of food always kills a fish | Yes, and she must never be allowed to keep a pet again | No, because fish never eat anything at all, ever
exercise: Sam asks his class to pray for his guinea pig Peanut. What is a fair way to do it? | Those who pray can pray; others have a quiet moment | Everyone must say the same prayer aloud | Nobody may mention Peanut at school | Only the Christian children may stay in the room for it
exercise: Lily's mum says Thumper has «gone to a better place». What does that phrase mean? | That Thumper has died | That Thumper ran away to a farm | That Thumper moved to another hutch | That Thumper is visiting friends
exercise: Can Lily visit the «better place»? | No, nobody can visit the dead, but she can visit his grave | Yes, by bus on Sunday afternoons | Yes, if she asks the vicar for a ticket | No, because rabbits are not allowed visitors
exercise: Kai's tortoise is very still in winter. What is the most likely reason? | It is hibernating, a deep winter rest | It has turned into stone for the winter | It is dead and will come back in spring | It is angry with Kai for something
exercise: How is hibernation different from death? | In hibernation the animal still breathes slowly and its heart beats | In hibernation the heart stops but restarts | There is no difference between them | Death lasts only for winter, like hibernation
exercise: Zara blames the neighbour for her stick insects dying. What is the wise move? | Ask calmly what happened; grief can come out as anger | Shout at the neighbour over the garden fence until she cries | Never speak to the neighbour again | Blame herself instead of anyone else
exercise: Harry asks if Captain went to fish heaven. What is the most honest answer? | Nobody knows for sure, but Captain was loved | Yes, there is a big pond in the sky for fish | No, fish are not real animals | Yes, and Captain can see us from there
exercise: What do many Hindus and Buddhists believe happens to animals after death? | They are reborn | They become stars | They turn into ghosts | They become people at once
exercise: What does the Bible tell Christians to do with people who are grieving? | Mourn with those who mourn | Tell them to cheer up quickly | Leave them alone until they stop crying | Remind them it was their fault
test: Ollie's little sister whispers, «Captain's just having a nap, innit?» What is the kind, true answer? | No, Captain has died; his body has stopped and he won't wake | Yes, fish nap like that every morning | Yes, wait until lunch and he'll swim | No, he is pretending to annoy us
test: A Gen Z teen says, «Flushing your fish is lowkey disrespectful, no cap.» Is there a good reason not to flush? | It can spread illness to wild fish, and burial feels kinder | Fish come back up the pipes if flushed | It is against the law to flush a fish in every single country in the world | Flushing makes the toilet go bad
test: Leo's mate from Wales says, «Aw, I'm tamping your brother said that, come 'ere, cwtch.» («Tamping» means «furious»; «cwtch» means «cuddle».) What is the mate doing? | Comforting Leo and agreeing his grief is real | Telling Leo off for crying | Agreeing it's just a bird | Asking Leo if he can have his budgie's old cage now
test: What would happen if Ava kept believing she killed her fish when she did not? | She would carry guilt that does not belong to her | She would become a much better swimmer than before | The fish would slowly come back to life in its bowl | Nothing at all, because feeling guilty never hurts anyone
test: Lily's uncle in Cork says, «Ah, Thumper's gone to a better place, pet, grand so.» («Grand» means «fine, all right».) What should Lily understand? | Thumper has died; the words are a gentle way to say it | Thumper is living with a better family | Thumper has gone on holiday to Cork | Thumper will be back home when the weather is grand again
test: Kai shouts, «Dad, Shelly the tortoise is dead, she won't move!» It is January. What should Kai do first? | Ask an adult or vet to check; she may be hibernating | Bury her in the garden straight away before going to school | Put her in a hot oven to wake her | Throw cold water on her to see
test: A girl in Zara's class says, «Your neighbour is well sus, she killed them on purpose.» («Sus» means «suspicious».) What is fair? | We don't know; the insects may have been ill, so ask calmly | Right, the neighbour must be punished | Right, neighbours always kill pets | Stick insects can't die, so the neighbour must have hidden them
test: A cartoon shows a dead goldfish with halo and harp in a «fish heaven» pond. What is the truthful note? | Nobody knows for sure what happens to animals; beliefs differ | This is exactly what happens to every fish | Fish heaven is in the Bible | Fish turn into angels after one week
test: Sam's teacher, formally: «Those who wish to pray for Peanut may do so; everyone else may keep a moment of quiet.» Why is this fair? | It lets each child share in their own way | It forces every single child in the class to pray | It forbids anyone at all to pray at school | It makes all the non-praying children leave the room
test: A Boston kid says, «It's wicked sad Nibbles died, did Chloe kill him?» («Wicked» means «very».) What is the honest answer? | No, he was old for a hamster and died peacefully at home | Yes, by going to school that day | Yes, hamsters only ever die if they are left alone in the house | No, the hamster is still alive in the bedding
test: «My nan says only people go to heaven, my mate says his dog will be there,» says Harry. Which is the best view? | Christians differ about animals, and nobody knows for sure | Nan is right and his mate is lying | His mate is right and Nan is lying | Heaven is only for dogs and other pets, not for people at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of a domestic fish discovered floating motionless at the surface of its tank? | It has died, and its bodily functions have ceased permanently | It is engaged in a period of ordinary sleep | It is hibernating and will resume activity in spring | It is feigning death in order to obtain food
exam: Identify the principal distinction between hibernation and death in a tortoise. | In hibernation respiration and heartbeat continue at a reduced rate | In hibernation the heart ceases to beat entirely | Hibernation is permanent whereas death is temporary | There exists no distinction whatsoever between them
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning religious belief as to the fate of animals after death. | Beliefs differ, and no person possesses certain knowledge thereof | All religions teach that animals ascend to heaven | All religions teach that animals cease to exist | It has been scientifically demonstrated that all animals are reborn
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression «gone to a better place» as employed in respect of a deceased pet. | That the animal has died | That the animal has been rehomed | That the animal has escaped | That the animal has been sold
